  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">TMEA Core Support - TradeMark East Africa Core Support</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">Trade Mark East Africa Core Support</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Program for Regional Economic Integration in East Africa focusing on  lowering trade and transport costs through trade facilitation (and    infrastructure) and capacity building. Program targets public sector, private sector and civil society.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">Program for regional ekonomisk integration i &#xd6;stafrika med fokus p&#xe5;   att s&#xe4;nka handels och transportkostnader genom                        handelsprocedurf&#xf6;renklingar och kapacitetsbyggnad. M&#xe5;lgruppen &#xe4;r      offentliga liksom private sektorn och civila samh&#xe4;llet.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">TradeMark East Africa (TMEA) is a DFID-created not-for-profit company that aims to strengthen regional integration and improve trade competitiveness in East Africa. It was set up in 2009 with offices in each of the East African Community (EAC) member states (Burundi, Kenya, Rwanda, South Sudan, Uganda, and Tanzania). From 2011 - 2017 it implemented Strategy 1, focusing on promoting trade along major transport corridors in East Africa.
The overarching goal of TMEA is to contribute to increased growth and reduced poverty in East Africa.
The purpose of the contribution, thus the impact objective is to support greater regional integration and trade competitiveness in East Africa. In order to reach this impact objective there are four separate outcome objectives:
-Reduction in transport and related costs along the key corridors in East Africa (35%)-EAC Institutions develop a comprehensive framework for regional Integration (20%)-Partner states substantially increase the implementation of a comprehensive framework for regional integration (30%)-Private sector and civil society positively influence regional integration policies and practices for growth in trade (15%)</narrative>
  </description>
